{
  "slug": "numeros",
  "number": 7,
  "part": 2,
  "title": "Numbers: rules and traps",
  "tagline": "The logic of 70 to 99, et, the s in vingts and cents, and how they change sound.",
  "goal": "Understand how numbers from 70 to 99 are built, know when they take et, a hyphen or an s, pronounce six, dix, huit and neuf correctly depending on what follows, and write figures the French way.",
  "minutes": 10,
  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "igual",
      "title": "What works like English",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "This module isn't a list of numbers: it assumes you know the basics and focuses on the **rules and traps**."
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "17 to 19 are built by adding: *dix-sept* (ten-seven), *dix-huit*, *dix-neuf*.",
            "Tens from 20 to 60 combine as in English: *trente-deux* (thirty-two), *quarante-cinq* (forty-five).",
            "*cent* and *mille* take no «one» in front: *cent euros* (a hundred euros), *mille ans* (a thousand years)."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "70-99",
      "title": "The logic of 70 to 99",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "From 70 onwards, French stops using new tens and **does sums** instead:"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "head": [
            "Number",
            "In French",
            "The sum"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "70",
              "*soixante-dix*",
              "60 + 10"
            ],
            [
              "71",
              "*soixante et onze*",
              "60 + 11"
            ],
            [
              "75",
              "*soixante-quinze*",
              "60 + 15"
            ],
            [
              "80",
              "*quatre-vingts*",
              "4 × 20"
            ],
            [
              "81",
              "*quatre-vingt-un*",
              "4 × 20 + 1"
            ],
            [
              "90",
              "*quatre-vingt-dix*",
              "4 × 20 + 10"
            ],
            [
              "97",
              "*quatre-vingt-dix-sept*",
              "4 × 20 + 10 + 7"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "tip",
          "text": "70–79: 60 + a number from 10 to 19. 80–99: 4 × 20 + something; from 90 to 99 that something also runs from 10 to 19: *quatre-vingt-douze* (92), *quatre-vingt-seize* (96). Think of the old English «four score»."
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "note",
          "text": "In Belgium and Switzerland people say *septante* (70) and *nonante* (90), and in parts of Switzerland *huitante* (80)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "et",
      "title": "When et appears",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "table",
          "head": [
            "With et",
            "Without et"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "21 *vingt et un*",
              "81 *quatre-vingt-un*"
            ],
            [
              "31 *trente et un*",
              "91 *quatre-vingt-onze*"
            ],
            [
              "41, 51, 61 *… et un*",
              "22, 32… *vingt-deux, trente-deux*"
            ],
            [
              "71 *soixante et onze*",
              "101 *cent un*"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "tip",
          "text": "*et* («and») only appears with 1 (and with the 11 of 71) after 20, 30, 40, 50 and 60. Never with 80 or 100."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "s",
      "title": "The s in vingts and cents",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "table",
          "head": [
            "Rule",
            "With s",
            "Without s"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "*vingt* takes an s only in exactly 80",
              "*quatre-vingts* (80)",
              "*quatre-vingt-trois* (83)"
            ],
            [
              "*cent* takes an s when multiplied and nothing follows",
              "*deux cents* (200)",
              "*deux cent dix* (210) · *cent* (100)"
            ],
            [
              "*mille* never changes",
              "—",
              "*deux mille, trois mille*"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "tip",
          "text": "The s only appears when the number **ends there**. If anything follows, it disappears."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "guiones",
      "title": "Hyphens",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "**Traditional spelling:** hyphens between the parts under 100, except with *et*: *dix-sept, quatre-vingt-dix, vingt et un*. No hyphen with *cent* and *mille*: *deux cent trente*.",
            "**Reformed spelling (1990):** hyphens everywhere: *vingt-et-un, deux-cent-trente*."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "note",
          "text": "Both are correct. Pick one and stick to it."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "pronunciacion",
      "title": "Numbers that change sound",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "table",
          "head": [
            "Number",
            "On its own",
            "+ consonant",
            "+ vowel"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "*six*",
              "«sees»",
              "«see» (*six livres*)",
              "«seez» (*six ans*)"
            ],
            [
              "*dix*",
              "«dees»",
              "«dee» (*dix livres*)",
              "«deez» (*dix ans*)"
            ],
            [
              "*huit*",
              "«weet»",
              "«wee» (*huit livres*)",
              "«weet» (*huit ans*)"
            ],
            [
              "*neuf*",
              "«nuhf»",
              "«nuhf» (*neuf livres*)",
              "«nuhv» only with *ans* and *heures*"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "warning",
          "title": "Vingt",
          "text": "On its own it sounds «van» (nasal), but from 21 to 29 the t is pronounced: *vingt-deux* = «vant-duh». From 80 to 99 it's silent again: *quatre-vingt-un* = «katr-van-uhn»."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "liaison",
      "title": "Liaison with numbers",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "**Required** before a vowel or silent h: *un‿ami, deux‿heures, trois‿enfants, six‿ans, dix‿élèves, vingt‿ans, cent‿ans*.",
            "**Forbidden** before *huit* and *onze*: *les │ huit jours, les │ onze joueurs, le │ onze novembre*."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "More details in [[14]]."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cifras",
      "title": "Writing figures",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "table",
          "head": [
            "Use",
            "English",
            "French"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Decimals",
              "3.50",
              "**3,50** (comma)"
            ],
            [
              "Thousands",
              "1,000",
              "**1 000** (space)"
            ],
            [
              "Prices",
              "€3.50",
              "**3,50 €** (symbol after, with a space)"
            ],
            [
              "Percentages",
              "20%",
              "**20 %** (with a space)"
            ],
            [
              "Times",
              "2:30 pm",
              "**14 h 30** (24-hour clock)"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "note",
          "title": "Phone numbers and prices",
          "text": "Phone numbers are read in pairs: 06 12 34 56 78 → *zéro six, douze, trente-quatre, cinquante-six, soixante-dix-huit*. Prices are said *trois euros cinquante*, usually without *centimes*."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
